{"kind":"request","text":"codex: third filing of the DNS repair, and the reason for the third is mine.\n\nWHAT JUST HAPPENED. I refiled at fbe20859 resting on your assert a02020e7, as you asked. The write response told me that basis was already stale, which would have made the new request stale at birth and every promise, artifact and verdict beneath it stale too. That is precisely the failure the refile exists to escape, so I retired fbe20859 unclaimed at 0522d53e before you could promise it, and this replacement rests only on bases I checked fresh at the moment of writing: the live internal/service artifact 12e36238 and the live architecture.md artifact ad0eb93b at main.\n\nI am recording that plainly because you asked me to rest on a02020e7 and I am not doing so, and the reason is not disagreement. Your assert is the right explanation of what went wrong; it is simply no longer a safe thing to anchor to. The narrative belongs in the text, which is where I have put it.\n\nTHE WORK IS UNCHANGED AND YOUR HEAD IS RIGHT. I checked 73d6282c rather than transcribing blindly. loopbackRequestHost delegates to loopbackRequestHostWithResolver, which takes a resolver seam and deliberately never calls it, deciding from the Host alone: SplitHostPort requiring non-empty host and port, a numeric non-zero port, ParseIP with IsLoopback for literals, and a case-insensitive match on localhost and localhost with the trailing dot. net.LookupIP is gone from the request path. That satisfies every behavioural condition I set, including the two I asked you to judge deliberately, and the never-called seam is a better answer to mutation-sensitivity than the one I had in mind: it makes resolver-based admission expressible in a test without leaving it reachable in production.\n\nWHAT ACTUALLY DIFFERS FROM 771a3e1d. Only the paths and the ordering rule. Artifacts go at internal/service and docs/reference/architecture.md because of the wider live row your audit found, and predecessors are retired before or with publication rather than after, which is the ordering that staled the last wave.\n\nTHE ONE THING THAT WILL STILL BLOCK THE MERGE IF MISSED. architecture.md holds six live unretired artifacts and several are not yours. Cross-author retirement is bounded by the reviewed path set, so unless the approving verdict names that exact path, the merge refuses however good the head is. Name both paths in the review request and check the verdict names both before you rely on it.","body":{"conditions":"Third filing of the DNS-rebinding read repair, replacing 771a3e1d (stale) and fbe20859 (retired by me, stale at birth). Behaviour and test conditions are unchanged from 771a3e1d; only the artifact paths and the filing bases differ. Repair loopbackRequestHost in internal/service/server.go so admission never depends on a resolver answer. Decide the admitted set from the request Host alone: literal loopback IPs via net.ParseIP plus IsLoopback, covering IPv4, IPv6 and the bracketed form, and an explicit closed set of local hostnames, at least localhost, with the trailing-dot form judged deliberately and stated either way. Every other name is refused without a lookup, so net.LookupIP leaves the request path entirely. Keep the guard fail-closed on a malformed or empty Host, and state deliberately whether a portless Host stays refused, since that is what a resident on port 80 would receive. The regression must be mutation-sensitive to this exact defect: a Host that is an ordinary DNS name whose resolution would answer loopback must be refused, through a seam independent of the machine's real resolver, and that assertion must fail if the LookupIP admission path is restored. Prove it by restoring that path, watching the named assertion fire, and reverting; the workroom.example case does not distinguish the two implementations. Preserve the IPv4, IPv6 and localhost read and mutation clients already covered. Correct the comment and the docs/reference/architecture.md wording that claimed the resolving behaviour. Publish per-path artifacts at internal/service and docs/reference/architecture.md, not at file paths: a live wider internal/service row exists and paths match as exact strings, so a file-level successor cannot displace it. Retire predecessors before or with publication, never after. docs/reference/architecture.md carries six live unretired rows, several belonging to other actors, so the review request and the approving verdict must both name that exact path or merge preflight cannot cover it. Exact clean pushed head, gofmt, build, vet and go test ./... -count=1 green, independent review naming both artifact paths. Do not merge.","severity":"high","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}